    <h1 data-split>A Guide to Music Industry Structure and Career Paths</h1>
    <p class="lede prose">How the music industry is structured, the roles from A&amp;R to distribution, and the skills each one actually needs.</p>
    <p class="links-line">Originally published in Traditional Chinese &middot; <a href="/writing">All writing</a></p>
    <div class="longform prose" style="margin-top:34px">
      <p class="rv">The music industry is bigger than singers and songwriters. If you want to work in it, this guide is for you. A lot of music lovers want to turn that passion into a career but don't know where to look. This piece covers record labels, music publishing, retail, copyright administration, and the skills each of those jobs actually needs.</p>
      <h2 class="rv">Introduction</h2>
      <p class="rv">I worked at a major music retail chain for over a year during university, then followed my degree into software engineering and spent four years there. Music never left me, so I went to the UK for a master's in the music industry to figure out where I could fit. The engineering years gave me project management experience, plus a habit of following startup culture and pushing my own self-improvement. This guide lays out how the industry is organized, what each role needs, and what the work actually involves.</p>
      <h2 class="rv">Overview of the Music Industry</h2>
      <blockquote class="rv prose">"The success of an artist or band is never achieved alone; it requires a dedicated behind-the-scenes team and the support of fans."  If you want to go fast, go alone; if you want to go far, go together.</blockquote>
      <p class="rv">The music industry makes money from music-related products and services: songwriting, technical production, business services, recording, publishing, physical distribution, live performance, digital streaming platforms, and copyright administration. Companies across these areas work closely together.</p>
      <p class="rv">The industry centers on the recording business, publishing, and live performance, which drive the peripheral music services around them. The historical "Big Six" record labels consolidated into today's "Big Three" (Universal Music Group, Sony Music, and Warner Music Group, ranked by global market share). As streaming replaced physical media and the recorded music market shrank, companies restructured. They split up services that used to run end to end under one roof: marketing, production, artist management, copyright administration, distribution, and outsourced each piece to cut costs and overhead. That gave independent labels more room in the market and gave artists, musicians, and producers more options. Major labels and independents now operate side by side.</p>

