      <h2 class="rv">Industry Roles, Job Descriptions, and Required Skills</h2>
      <h3 class="rv">Artist Manager</h3>
      <p class="rv">Experienced artist managers do more than plan a career. They deal with lawyers, accountants, record labels, publishers, and concert promoters, and that takes adaptability and structured problem-solving.</p>
      <p class="rv">Managers fall into three categories:</p>
      <ul class="now-list" style="max-width:none"><li><span class="dot"></span><span>Freelancers: Often former employees of music companies who have industry contacts and access. They start independent agencies or studios and scout emerging talent across online platforms, television, and film.</span></li><li><span class="dot"></span><span>Agency Employees: Staff within an agency or a major music company's in-house management department. They use established business networks to coordinate artist engagements in line with company strategy.</span></li><li><span class="dot"></span><span>Close Contacts: People artists trust and bring in once they're established enough to want familiar faces around them.</span></li></ul>
      <h4 class="rv">Responsibilities</h4>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Coordinate artist engagements, work with commercial partners, and develop new business.</li><li>Manage the artist's owned media and official social channels to build their public image.</li><li>Develop creative concepts, project proposals, and marketing campaigns.</li></ol>
      <h4 class="rv">Required Skills</h4>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Strong interpersonal communication, high emotional intelligence, and tact.</li><li>Proposal writing and creative planning to pitch campaigns that fit current trends.</li><li>Project execution, including timeline management, budget planning, and risk assessment.</li><li>Problem-solving and crisis management, with backup plans ready to keep projects on schedule.</li></ol>
      <p class="rv">A manager is also a friend, someone who checks in on the artist's mental and physical state. Artists carry pressure from public life that outsiders can't fully imagine, so part of the job is noticing that toll, adjusting plans, and making sure the artist gets rest and space. Balancing career moves against that well-being takes situational judgment and adaptability on top of the planning.</p>
      <h3 class="rv">A&amp;R (Artist and Repertoire)</h3>
      <p class="rv">A record label's core assets are its artists and master recording copyrights. An A&amp;R professional picks out market-ready talent and sounds from a flood of submissions. Beyond musical instinct, they need the planning skills to launch an artist. A solid grasp of copyright and licensing contracts matters too: A&amp;R coordinates song acquisition between record labels and music publishers, and the two hold different sets of rights.</p>
      <h4 class="rv">Responsibilities</h4>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Act as the main point of contact between the label and the artist to support how the artist is positioned in the market.</li><li>Work with music publishers to find the right songwriters and producers.</li><li>Scout emerging talent and oversee artist development and musical training.</li><li>Manage music production, including budgeting, scheduling, and song selection.</li><li>Research the market and analyze social media to find target audiences and trends.</li><li>Handle the paperwork behind recording contracts and copyright clearances.</li></ol>
      <h4 class="rv">Required Skills</h4>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Sharp artistic and musical judgment, with strong critical listening skills.</li><li>Cross-functional communication and emotional intelligence.</li><li>Proposal writing and structured execution.</li><li>Project management: budgets, scheduling, risk.</li><li>A solid understanding of music copyright and standard industry contracts.</li></ol>
      <h3 class="rv">Marketing, Promotion, Sales, and Distribution Specialists</h3>
      <p class="rv">Depending on company structure, marketing, promotion, and sales may be combined into a single department or managed separately. Distribution means getting products into physical and digital retail channels.</p>
      <h4 class="rv">Responsibilities</h4>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Write project proposals and campaign concepts.</li><li>Run official social media to build the brand's and the artist's visibility.</li><li>Coordinate with vendors and manufacturing partners on pricing and physical release formats.</li><li>Develop retail channels, manage inventory (for distribution roles), and work with retail buyers.</li></ol>
      <h4 class="rv">Required Skills</h4>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Cross-departmental communication and coordination.</li><li>The ability to pitch a proposal well enough to get artists and executives behind a campaign strategy.</li><li>An eye for market trends and a knack for creative storytelling.</li><li>Basic design skills (Photoshop, Illustrator) to review specs and assets with production vendors.</li><li>A head for numbers: inventory, costs, budgets.</li></ol>
      <h3 class="rv">Publishing Copyright Specialist</h3>
      <p class="rv">Most copyright specialists work at music publishing companies. Their core responsibilities:</p>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Matching songwriters and composers with commercial licensing opportunities.</li><li>Administering copyrights and royalty accounting.</li><li>Scouting songwriting talent, negotiating publishing agreements, and managing commission splits.</li></ol>
      <p class="rv">Their main counterparts are lyricists, composers, and commercial clients. Depending on company size, publishing companies may also have dedicated A&amp;R roles.</p>
      <h4 class="rv">Responsibilities</h4>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Administer music rights and calculate royalty distributions.</li><li>Work with commercial clients on sync licensing fees and composer placements.</li><li>Draft and execute publishing agreements with songwriters.</li></ol>
      <h4 class="rv">Required Skills</h4>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Familiarity with music copyright frameworks and publishing contract structures.</li><li>Proficiency in document and database management tools.</li><li>Detail-oriented and comfortable with numbers.</li></ol>
      <h3 class="rv">Music Retail</h3>
      <p class="rv">The distribution phase places a release's inventory into physical and digital channels: Chia Chia Record (佳佳唱片), Books.com.tw (博客來), independent record stores, Spotify, and Apple Music.</p>
      <p class="rv">Physical retail runs on purchasing specialists who coordinate with distributors to order inventory. In-store staff manage how products are presented and curate selections to communicate an album's value to listeners. From my own year working at a record store, every staff member needs to know at least one genre well: I knew rock and indie bands, the store manager knew Japanese music, and a senior colleague knew Western pop. Staff also need a general sense of music history and new releases.</p>
      <h4 class="rv">Responsibilities</h4>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Develop thematic merchandising and sales campaigns.</li><li>Manage inventory, stock replenishment, and returns.</li><li>Handle retail operations, point-of-sale transactions, and customer service.</li></ol>
      <h4 class="rv">Required Skills</h4>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Campaign planning and execution tailored to music trends.</li><li>Solid understanding of inventory metrics and sales data.</li><li>Deep knowledge of at least one music genre to provide customer recommendations.</li><li>Strong interpersonal communication and customer service skills.</li></ol>
      <h3 class="rv">Digital Streaming</h3>
      <p class="rv">Streaming platforms hire specialized technical talent: iOS, Android, backend, software, data, and QA engineers. Beyond building and maintaining the platform, these companies also employ copyright specialists, project managers, marketing leads, and public relations staff to expand content and build the brand.</p>
      <h2 class="rv">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3 class="rv">How do you break into these companies?</h3>
      <p class="rv">In Taiwan, getting into a mainstream record label or management agency comes down to who you know. Partly that's because so many casual fans apply, and partly it's because managers and artists need a close working relationship. Trust and personal fit drive these hires, the same way referrals drive hiring in overseas markets.</p>
      <p class="rv">If you want to build a career abroad, markets like the US, Japan, and the UK care more about what you can do than who you know. If you're applying to UK roles after working in Taiwan, a reference from a former manager counts as a real endorsement.</p>
      <p class="rv">Independent labels are easier to get into than the majors. KAO!INC. (顏社), for example, posts openings on their own social channels for people with a background in project management or marketing. Music retail is another accessible entry point, as long as you can show domain knowledge and put together a professional resume.</p>
      <p class="rv">Accessible entry points:</p>
      <ul class="now-list" style="max-width:none"><li><span class="dot"></span><span>Retail Channels: Chia Chia Record (佳佳唱片), Eslite Music (誠品音樂), Five Music (五大唱片), Kuang Nan (光南大批發), White Wabbit Records (小白兔唱片), 38C Audio (38度C).</span></li><li><span class="dot"></span><span>Independent and Non-Major Labels: KAO!INC. (顏社), Linfair Records (福茂唱片), Wind Music (風潮音樂), A Good Day Records (風和日麗), Trapped Records (角頭音樂), Good Sense Music (好感音樂).</span></li></ul>
      <h3 class="rv">Why are independent distributors, live events, and CMOs excluded?</h3>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Independent digital distribution platforms, like CD Baby, are still new to Taiwan and have few local offices.</li><li>Live performance production in Taiwan runs differently than it does overseas. Technical production companies like B'in Live (必應創造) recruit concert planners, lighting designers, and hardware engineers through platforms like 104 Job Bank (104人力銀行).</li><li>Collective Management Organisations (CMOs) in Taiwan are mostly quasi-public entities. They hire through civil service exams or fixed-term contracts, which offer less long-term stability unless it fits your specific career plans.</li></ol>
      <p class="rv">Related reading: <a href="/writing/indie-artists-overseas-tour-guide">Seven practical considerations for independent artists touring overseas</a></p>
      <h3 class="rv">Is it a problem if I do not listen to much music?</h3>
      <p class="rv">It depends on the role. For marketing, planning, and publishing, you should know at least one genre in depth and keep up with the charts and market trends. For A&amp;R, you need to listen across a wide range of genres and have real context on the industry.</p>
      <h3 class="rv">What if I do not have any of these skills yet?</h3>
      <p class="rv">Publishing technical or analytical writing online is one of the best ways to build domain expertise. Take in industry information, work through it, and write down a clear point of view. That builds the structured thinking and communication skills any job needs.</p>
      <p class="rv">For example, you could write music reviews or industry analyses focused on a specific era or subgenre. You can also build multimedia content around your own name. Run a project from start to finish, find the bottlenecks, and solve problems on your own. That teaches you the marketing, planning, and operational skills the job actually needs.</p>
      <h2 class="rv">Conclusion</h2>
      <p class="rv">Companies hire for three reasons: replacing someone, covering a growing workload, or bringing in a specific technical skill, and whoever gets the job needs to be ready to work right away. In smaller markets like Taiwan, companies want people who already have concrete, transferable skills. Build those skills on purpose, and put together a resume that makes your background easy to follow. That's what gets you in the door.</p>
